## 3. PART 1 — Admin Upload: "How does knowledge get INTO the bot?"

### 3.1 API entry point

```
POST /api/ai/documents/upload
Authorization: Bearer <admin-or-registrar-JWT>
multipart/form-data:  file, doc_type, department, semester, academic_year
```

The endpoint (`backend/ai_engine/api/document_routes.py`) does **3 things**:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant A as Admin UI
    participant R as POST /api/ai/documents/upload
    participant S as DocumentService.upload_and_index
    participant T as FastAPI BackgroundTasks
    participant P as pipeline (extract→clean→chunk→embed)
    participant C as ChromaDB

    A->>R: file + doc_type("timetable") + department("cse")
    R->>R: _require_admin(current_user) → 403 for non-admin
    R->>R: save bytes to tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile
    R->>T: background_tasks.add_task(_process_and_cleanup)
    T->>S: upload_and_index(temp_path, filename, doc_type, dept, sem)
    S->>S: validate doc_type ∈ DOC_TYPES · validate ext ∈ [.pdf .docx .txt .csv .md]
    S->>S: copy to uploads/ai_documents/<filename> (dedupe → _1, _2 ...)
    S->>P: index_document_from_path(...)
    P->>C: upsert_chunks(chunks, metadatas)
    T->>T: finally: delete temp file (no disk leak)
    R-->>A: 200 {message: "Document upload started", status: "processing"}
```

> **Why so fast?** The API returns immediately with `status: "processing"` and
> the heavy indexing runs in a **background task** (a large PDF can take
> 30–60 s). The temp file is deleted in a `finally` block regardless of success.

### 3.2 Step-by-step ingestion

Each step lives in `backend/ai_engine/document_pipeline/`:

| Step | Module | What really happens |
|---|---|---|
| **1. Extract** | `extractor.py` | Dispatch on extension: **PDF** → PyMuPDF page text (reading-order, multi-column); **DOCX** → python-docx paragraphs **+ full table rendering**; TXT/CSV/MD → stdlib parsers. Output: `[(page_no, text), ...]` |
| **2. Clean** | `cleaner.py` | 10-step normalization (bullets, whitespace, URLs, unicode fixes…). **Deliberately keeps the table sentinel markers** `\u0001TABLE…\u0002` so tables are not destroyed |
| **3. Chunk** | `chunker.py` | Prose → `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` (768 tokens, 100 overlap). **Table blocks** → row-groups of `TABLE_ROWS_PER_CHUNK = 3` rows, header line repeated in each group |
| **4. Embed** | `embeddings/model.py` | `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` → **384-dim vector** per chunk. Runs **locally on CPU** (~5 ms/chunk, no API cost). Singleton, loaded once at startup |
| **5. Store** | `vectorstore/manager.py` | `upsert_chunks(chunks, metadatas)` into ChromaDB collection `campus_genie_docs` |

Every chunk is stored with **metadata** (this powers the answer's citations and
the department/semester filter):

```json
{
  "source_file": "KRMU_Master_Timetable_RAG_Test_v2.docx",
  "doc_type": "timetable",
  "department": "cse",
  "semester": 1,
  "academic_year": "2025-26",
  "chunk_index": 12,
  "document_id": "…uuid…"
}
```

### 3.3 WORKED EXAMPLE — uploading a timetable DOCX

Real transformation inside the extractor. Raw DOCX table rows are semantically
**useless** for vector search — `Wednesday | 10:00 AM - 11:00 AM | Research
Methodology | Dr. … | C-104` embeds as a meaningless blob. The extractor rewrites
every row into a **self-describing labeled sentence**:

```
BEFORE (raw table cell values):
------------------------------------------------
| Wednesday | 10:00–11:00 | Research Methodology | Dr. Anita Sharma | C-104   |
| Thursday  | 09:00–10:00 | DBMS Lab             | Dr. Neha Bhatia   | C-302   |
------------------------------------------------

AFTER (what actually gets embedded):
------------------------------------------------
TABLE 1 (Columns: Day | Time | Subject / Course | Faculty | Room):
Day: Wednesday, Time: 10:00–11:00, Subject / Course: Research Methodology, Faculty: Dr. Anita Sharma, Room: C-104
Day: Thursday, Time: 09:00–10:00, Subject / Course: DBMS Lab, Faculty: Dr. Neha Bhatia, Room: C-302
------------------------------------------------
```

**How the extractor builds this** (`_render_table_with_headers`):

1. `_extract_table_rows` → collapse merged-cell duplicates (a cell repeated across
   columns is skipped).
2. `_looks_like_header_row` → finds the header row using `_TIME_RE`
   (`^\d{1,2}:\d{2}`), day-name detection (`monday…sunday`), and `_ROOM_RE`.
3. The header row becomes **column labels**; every data row is emitted as
   `Label: value, Label: value, …`.
4. The rendered table is wrapped in sentinels:
   `\u0001TABLE … \u0002`.

The chunker then splits the marked block on the sentinels and groups rows
**3 at a time**, repeating the header line at the top of each group — so a
38-row timetable becomes 13 searchable row-group chunks (each chunk stays
semantically focused).

### 3.4 Where the chunks live

```
backend/chroma_db/          ← persistent ChromaDB (gitignored)
  └─ campus_genie_docs      ← collection name (config CHROMA_COLLECTION_NAME)
```

Current live KB: **581 chunks across 12 documents** (health check
`GET /api/ai/health` reports `chromadb.chunks`). Examples by source:

| Source file | Chunks |
|---|---|
| MCA 2025-27 Handbook_Core (23-09-2025).pdf | 219 |
| MCA 2025-27 Handbook (23-09-25) AI ML.pdf | 188 |
| KRMU_Hostel_Residential_Life_Policy_RAG_v1.docx | 40 |
| KRMU_Attendance_Policy_RAG_Knowledge_Base_v3.docx | 37 |
| KRMU_Master_Timetable_RAG_Test_v2.docx | 17 |
| … 7 more | … |
| **Total** | **581** |

> Count changes as admin uploads/deletes documents — it is **not** static.

### 3.5 Managing / reindexing the knowledge base

| Action | Endpoint / command | Internal behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| List docs | `GET /api/ai/documents` | returns metadata of every indexed doc |
| Stats | `GET /api/ai/documents/stats` | chunk counts |
| Delete | `DELETE /api/ai/documents/{filename}` | `delete_by_source_file` removes all its chunks |
| **Reindex** | `python scripts/reindex_knowledge_base.py` | clears collection → re-scans `uploads/ai_documents` → skips a **skip-list** (synthetic RAG-test docs + stray reports) → infers `doc_type` from filename keywords (timetable/attendance/policy/notice/admission/faculty/scholarship/placement/handbook) → dedupes `_1`/`_2` suffix duplicates → re-indexes |

