## 4. PART 2 — Chat Query: "How does the bot find an ANSWER?"

### 4.1 The full request path

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant S as Student (chat widget)
    participant R as POST /api/ai/conversations/{id}/messages
    participant CC as ChatService.send_message
    participant G as LangGraph run_agent
    participant C as ChromaDB
    participant M as MySQL
    participant L as Gemini (llm/client.py)

    S->>R: "When is the Advanced Database Management Systems class?"
    R->>R: JWT → must be STUDENT · conversation belongs to user
    R->>CC: send_message(...)
    CC->>CC: rate limit (60/min · 20 burst · 1000/day)
    CC->>CC: injection scan + PII masking
    CC->>G: initial AgentState (user_context, trace_id)

    G->>G: 1 · load_memory  → last 6 turns
    G->>G: 2 · classify_intent → timetable_query, needs_retrieval=TRUE, needs_tool=?
    par parallel
        G->>C: 3a · retrieve_context (multi-query + rerank)
        G->>M: 3b · tool_call (live DB tools)
    end
    G->>L: 4 · generate_answer (RAG prompt = context + tools + history)
    L-->>G: JSON {answer, confidence, sources, follow_up_questions}
    G->>M: 5 · save_memory (user + AI messages)
    G-->>CC: final AgentState
    CC-->>R: rich ai_message
    R-->>S: rendered: answer + confidence + citations + follow-ups
```

### 4.2 WORKED EXAMPLE — "When is the Advanced Database Management Systems class?"

Let's trace this exact real question through the pipeline with the actual
constants from the code.

**Step 1 — Intent classification**

`classify_intent_node` first tries the fast keyword router
(`fast_intent.py`). The message contains *"class"* and *"Advanced Database
Management Systems"* — hits `_SCHEDULE_KEYWORDS`, and because
`FAST_INTENT_ROUTING=True` it is classified **locally** (no LLM call) as
`TIMETABLE_QUERY` → `needs_retrieval=True`.

> For harder questions the router falls back to Gemini with a structured JSON
> prompt, then the result is sanitized into a `ClassificationResult`.

**Step 2 — Build the search query list**

`retrieve_context_node` assembles **up to ~8 queries** by concatenating the
refined query, the raw user message, curriculum enrichment, and **schedule
enrichment**:

| # | Query (actually sent to ChromaDB) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `when is the advanced database management systems class` | refined retrieval query |
| 2 | `When is the Advanced Database Management Systems class?` | raw user message |
| 3 | `timetable schedule Day Time Subject Course Faculty Room` | schedule template |
| 4 | `Day: Time: Subject / Course: Faculty: Room: timetable` | label-vocabulary probe |
| 5 | `Code: Course Title: Type: Credits: Faculty: Room: timetable` | label-vocabulary probe |
| 6 | `Master Academic Timetable Course Title Faculty Room Credits Day` | timetable probe |
| 7 | `Course Title: Advanced Database Management Systems Faculty: Room: Day: Time:` | subject detected via `_extract_subject_phrase` |
| 8 | `timetable Advanced Database Management Systems class schedule day faculty room` | direct subject probe |

These come from `_schedule_enrichment_queries` — triggered because the message
contains a schedule keyword (`class`) and/or a weekday. `_extract_subject_phrase`
strips filler words and returns the longest noun run:
**`"Advanced Database Management Systems"`**.

**Step 3 — Metadata filter**

ChromaDB search is constrained to `(department == "all" OR department == <user's>)`
and `(semester == 0 OR semester == <user's>)` when the student context has that
data. The timetable doc is tagged `department: cse`, so it passes.

**Step 4 — Multi-query vector search**

Each query is embedded (384-dim) and runs `search(top_k=15)` against ChromaDB.
Results are merged **by chunk_id**, keeping the best similarity score per chunk,
then sorted by similarity. (If the filtered search returns nothing, it retries
**without** the filter.)

**Step 5 — Cross-encoder reranking**

All candidates are re-scored by the `cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2`
reranker, which reads *query + document pairs together* (much stronger than
embedding cosine). The full ordering is kept — not a truncated top-N — so the
hybrid step can use every rank.

**Step 6 — Hybrid score + table-row bonus**

Each candidate gets a blended score (see [4.3](#43-the-hybrid-score-decoded)),
and timetable rows get the subject-gated bonus:

```python
if schedule_queries and _looks_like_table_row(content):      # ≥3 label hits
    if not subject_tokens:                                    # no subject detected
        bonus = 0.22
    else:
        frac = len(content_tokens ∩ subject_tokens) / len(subject_tokens)
        if frac >= 0.5:                                       # row really is about DBMS
            bonus = 0.22 * frac
```

A row containing `Advanced Database Management Systems` with `Dr. Neha Bhatia`,
`C-302`, `Monday ... 09:00–10:00` gets `frac ≈ 1.0 → bonus ≈ 0.22`, which is
enough to beat similar-but-wrong rows. Rows about other subjects get **no bonus**
— they cannot crowd out the answer.

**Step 7 — Sibling expansion**

The top-6 anchors pull their neighbours (±2 chunks) into the final set at a
small penalty (−0.05), so a "Master Timetable" heading chunk brings every
neighbouring row-group along. Final cut: **top-8** (`RERANK_TOP_N`).

**Step 8 — Answer generation**

The top-8 `RankedDocument`s become the `RETRIEVED CONTEXT` block of the RAG
prompt (each bounded to 1200 chars), alongside tool results and history. Gemini
(`gemini-3.5-flash`, fallback rotation) returns strict JSON, which is parsed and
returned.

**The real output this question produced:**

> **Advanced Database Management Systems (DBMS)** classes are held:
> - **Monday** 09:00–10:00 — **Dr. Neha Bhatia** — **C-302**
> - **Wednesday** 10:00–11:00 — Dr. Neha Bhatia — C-302
> - **Friday** 09:00–10:00 — Dr. Neha Bhatia — C-302
>
> *According to [KRMU_Master_Timetable_RAG_Test_v2.docx]* — confidence: 1.0

And the debug log confirmed the fix is active:
`answer.generate.start → docs_count: 16, has_tool_result: true`
(16 real chunks reached the generator — before the state-merge fix it was `0`).

### 4.3 The hybrid score, decoded

From `retriever.py::_hybrid_score` (quality mode):

```
score = 0.5·(1 − sim_rank/total)     # where the chunk landed in similarity order
      + 0.3·(1 − rerank_rank/total)  # where it landed in cross-encoder order
      + 0.2·lexical_overlap          # best overlap of query tokens & content
      + table_row_bonus              # ≤ 0.22 for subject-matched timetable rows
```

**Motivation:** nobody alone is reliable.

- Similarity-only: misses tabular/list chunks (tables embed badly).
- Rerank-only: can drop good semantic matches that read poorly as text.
- Lexical overlap: catches label-row matches (`Day:`, `Time:`, `Room:`) even when
  the semantic embedding is mediocre.

In **fast mode** the reranker is skipped and weights become `0.7` similarity,
`0.0` rerank, `0.3` lexical.

### 4.4 Why tables used to fail (and the fix)

This is the project's central bug story:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph OLD["BEFORE (broken)"]
        O1["raw docx table: Wednesday | 10:00 | ..."] --> O2["embedding is a weak blob"]
        O2 --> O3["vector search can't match 'when is DBMS?'"]
        O3 --> O4["timetable chunks never retrieved → chats answer 'no information'"]
    end
    subgraph NEW["AFTER (fixed)"]
        N1["labeled rows: Day: Wednesday, Time: ..., Subject: DBMS ..."] --> N2["embedding + lexical overlap match the question"]
        N2 --> N3["schedule enrichment queries probe the label vocabulary"]
        N3 --> N4["subject-gated +0.22 row bonus surfaces the exact row"]
        N4 --> N5["top-8 evidence → grounded cited answer"]
    end
```

The fix has **five** parts working together:

1. **`table_markers.py`** — shared sentinel control chars `\u0001TABLE` / `\u0002`.
2. **`extractor.py`** — docx tables → labeled self-describing rows + header line;
   merged-cell dedup; header-row heuristics (`_TIME_RE`, `_DAY_NAMES`, `_ROOM_RE`).
3. **`chunker.py`** — table blocks split into **3-row chunks** (chosen by
   measurement: 3 rows keeps `≈0.64` embedding similarity to the subject probe,
   vs `0.50` at 8 rows of dilution).
4. **`retriever.py`** — `_schedule_enrichment_queries` + `_extract_subject_phrase`
   + `_SCHEDULE_KEYWORDS`/`_SCHEDULE_FILLER_WORDS` + the `0.22` subject-gated
   `_TABLE_ROW_BONUS`.
5. **`agent_state.py`** — `_keep_first_real` reducer so parallel
   `retrieve_context` ∥ `tool_call` branches can't clobber real documents with a
   stale `None` (that was the final reason answers arrived with 0 docs).

**Result:** the full timetable battery passes — 16/16 timetable questions return
the exact rows; non-schedule questions are unchanged.

### 4.5 Answer generation & the 12 rules

`generate_answer_node` special-cases:

| Condition | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `intent == GREETING` | canned welcome, no confidence bar |
| `no docs AND no tool result` | "I don't have that specific information in my knowledge base. Please contact the relevant department directly." |
| `confidence < 0.6` | low-confidence warning prepended |
| otherwise | RAG prompt → Gemini → strict JSON |

The RAG prompt (`prompts/rag_prompt.py`) has **12 strict rules**. Highlights:

| Rule | Content |
|---|---|
| 1 | Answer **exclusively** from provided context + tool results |
| 3 | **Never invent** dates, marks, percentages, deadlines, names, phones, room numbers, fees |
| 4 | **Always cite** exact filenames — `According to [filename]` |
| 5 | Tool (live DB) data **beats** document context on conflict |
| 12 | The "I don't know" response is the **last resort** — any partial grounding must be answered from |

The model must return **one strict JSON object**:

```json
{
  "answer": "**Advanced Database Management Systems (DBMS)** classes are held on
             Monday 09:00–10:00, Wednesday 10:00–11:00 and Friday 09:00–10:00,
             taught by Dr. Neha Bhatia in room C-302.
             According to [KRMU_Master_Timetable_RAG_Test_v2.docx].",
  "confidence": 1.0,
  "sources": ["KRMU_Master_Timetable_RAG_Test_v2.docx"],
  "follow_up_questions": ["Does DBMS have a lab session?", "Who teaches Data Structures?"]
}
```

The output survives a **defensive JSON parser** (markdown fences stripped,
trailing commas removed, first `{…}` object extracted, unterminated JSON closed).

> **LLM resilience:** every Gemini call goes through `llm/client.py`, which on
> `429` retries the same model once (per-minute cap), then **rotates through**
> `[gemini-flash-latest, gemini-flash-lite-latest, gemini-3.1-flash-lite]`, then
> falls back to the OpenRouter HTTP endpoint — and every attempt has a 60 s hard
> timeout. The chatbot **never hangs or fails silently**.

---

## 5. Glossary

| Term | Meaning (in this project) |
|---|---|
| Chunk | One searchable unit of text (~3 table rows or ~768 tokens of prose) |
| Embedding | 384-dim vector from `all-MiniLM-L6-v2`; semantic similarity = cosine |
| Rerank | Cross-encoder scores query+chunk pairs together — smarter than embeddings |
| Hybrid score | Weighted blend: similarity 0.5 · rerank 0.3 · lexical 0.2 (+ table bonus) |
| Table-row bonus | `+0.22` boost for labeled timetable rows matching the question's subject |
| Sentinel markers | `\u0001TABLE … \u0002` — protect table blocks from the cleaner & mark them for the chunker |
| `RERANK_TOP_N` | 8 — evidence chunks finally sent to the LLM |
| `RETRIEVAL_TOP_K` | 15 — candidates pulled out of ChromaDB before reranking |
| Sibling expansion | top-6 anchors pull in their immediate neighbours (±2 chunks) |
| `DocumentMetadata` | source_file, doc_type, department, semester, chunk_index, … stored per chunk |

